welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Professor Paul Saban associate  professor of history his research and  teaching focus on energy and  environmental history today we'll talk  with Professor Sabin about his new book  the bet Paul Ehrlich Julian Simon and  our gamble over Earth's future it  provides a framework for understanding  today's environmental debates  welcome professor saiva thanks so much  for having me let's start with an  overview of the book and of course tell  us the story about the bet sure well the  the book is uses an iconic bet between  the biologist Paul Ehrlich who is the  author of the 1968 blockbuster the  Population Bomb and and one of his main  critics a man named Julian Simon and  economist and they actually made a bet  in 1980 about five metals and their  prices and whether they would go up or  down over the course of the next ten  years and this was a proxy for their  arguments about overpopulation and  whether the world was headed for famine  resource scarcity war thermonuclear war  or or disease outbreaks and these have  been some of the predictions that  Ehrlich had made and Simon had argued in  fact that human ingenuity and innovation  would lead to overpopulation or  population growth not being ultimately a  problem okay so so science will alert  Simon a bit and and ever since people  arguing about what this what the outcome  of the bet means so he the the five  metals their copper a chromium nickel  tin and tungsten may declined pretty  much across the board Ehrlich ultimately  sent Simon a check for five hundred and  seventy six dollars to represent his the  decline in value of the the bundle of  metals that they had that they had bet  on and so the question is what does this  bet bet mean I mean metal prices aren't  necessarily the best proxy for the state  of humanity or the environmental  condition I was curious as to why they  chose metals I mean you know what is  relevancy of them right well I mean if  you situated in the late 1970s and think  of  about the the run-up in oil prices and  the oil shocks at that time and there  also was significant increase in prices  of metals at that time so there there  was a lot of focus on commodities and  commodity prices and the idea that there  we were headed towards scarcity so there  have been predictions in the early 70s  that we were gonna run out of oil during  the 1980s and that turned out not to be  the case  so so so the part of why I was  interested in this in this story coming  at it from an environmental history  perspective was that Simon won the bet  and trying to understand you know what  can we learn from from his victory over  overall look in this case I mean just to  expound on on what led you to write the  book I mean so how did you I mean is  this bet well known yeah well it has  become a significant touchstone for  particularly for conservatives  criticizing environmentalists over  environmental regulation actually the  Competitive Enterprise Institute offers  an award each year the Julian Simon  award for for innovators for for people  who focus on human innovation and and  the award trophy is a leaf that has the  five medals that were from the bet so I  guess it's sort of symbolic of how  Simon's victory has been a touchstone  and so it's seeing that if that these  predictions of population growth we're  perhaps mistaken then that also means  the various other kinds of environmental  concerns also maybe our mistake and  that's that's been the argument you must  in in terms of writing this book you  know looked back and have seen some  predictions back in the day that were  made that didn't necessarily pan out was  there anything that was particularly  surprising to you well I was surprised  and so to see how widely held some of  these predictions were about that we  were gonna run out of oil or in the late  60s this tremendous fears of worldwide  famine that was about to strike the  planet and and then some of the some of  the claims that were made based on that  also were striking us there was a book  that was published in the late 60s  called famine 1975 that argued that  there's going to be worldwide famines as  a result of overpopulation and food  shortages and and therefore we should  practice tree  and some some countries were seen as  being kind of beyond the pale unsavable  and that we should cut them off from  food aid and so some of some of the  arguments were pretty extreme in terms  of what we needed to do to avoid the  future crises it must have been somewhat  amusing to to have read some of them  interesting to see these predictions and  and and how they haven't turned out as  expected I mean so my book is called the  bet because of this little bit but it's  also another meaning of the title is  that we're all engaged in the bet today  about the future and so some of these  predictions haven't come true to date  but it doesn't mean that difficult times  or major challenges won't face us in the  future so I guess many of these issues  aren't entirely resolved so what do we  face in the future  well I'm most interested in the debate  over climate change right now and seeing  how that's unfolding and one of the  things that I think is interesting about  this earlier story about population  growth and resource scarcity is how the  debate today over climate change is kind  of stuck in ruts that were established  by the earlier fight between Simon and  Ehrlich and their allies and so you see  today accusations of alarmism about  about the climate on one side and great  skepticism about science by many  conservatives and part of that is drawn  from their previous criticisms over  about population growth than resource  scarcity now those things didn't turn  out the way that was expected but I  think climate change in fact is  happening and is a grave concern and  it's really unfortunate that we're stuck  in these these long-standing grunts yeah  I mean I have read that pretty much most  scientists will agree that there is  climate change going on that it is  happening so do you argue in your book  something along the lines of how - you  know what we need to do well I mean the  argument of the book is is one that we  need to recognize how these two extreme  perspectives in some ways produced each  other and and that and then also we need  to figure out what can we learn from  both sides so I guess I see that both  Ehrlich's perspective and Simon's  arguments have important insights to  share or that yes so Ehrlich's on on the  way that humans are transforming the  planet the tremendous impact that we  have many of the biological changes that  are unfolding but Simon's about the  adaptability of human society of  flexibility of human economies the role  of innovation and technology and science  all those things that that that mean  that we aren't on a straight line I  guess from abundance to scarcity as many  people who are predicting we were gonna  run out of various resources they said  we were gonna go you know from abundant  oil all of a sudden to scarcity but in  fact it's really a history of boom and  bust over over over the X of the whole  history of the oil economy and and so I  guess learning from both sides and I  think that's one of the essential points  in terms of learning from Julian Simon's  point of view he argued that problems  often led to solutions that ultimately  led us better off and my own view about  the climate story is that it's only by  recognizing that climate change is a  problem that we can unleash the  solutions that Julian Simon celebrated  and so one of the problems that we face  right now is that in their reluctance to  recognize that climate change is a  problem many of Simon's Ally  intellectual allies are in some ways  undermining the very kinds of solutions  that Simon celebrated well wouldn't  couldn't one argue that there are  certain people who recognize that  there's climate change but the people  who don't want to recognize it have a  financial gain so that's part of the  problem isn't it well it is true that  there are powerful financial interests  actually on all sides of this of this  story I mean and but my my book is more  about the intellectual battle and and  recognize an exit there are important  insights on both sides it's not just a  financial fight I mean they're there  that is very important the oil oil  fossil fuel industries are very powerful  and have deep vested interest in in  sustaining the fossil fuel economy but  at the same time the whole debate is  actually embedded in this larger clash  over how we think about the future  mm-hmm okay so how do you think we  should think about the future  well I'm cautiously optimistic about  about the future recognizing that we  have major challenges ahead but that I  think if we if we if we take the steps  necessary to address them we can  overcome them and  the case of climate change the major the  major steps would be bringing bringing  climate into the into the marketplace  actually to the extent through through  carbon taxes or through kind of  regulatory systems like cap-and-trade  but that there'd be some way to put the  power of human innovation and and  markets to bear on the on the carbon  dioxide the climate problem and that  really what the problem we have right  now is that it's it's sort of  free-floating outside of the marketplace  and there's no way to bring our the  powerful forces we have to bear on  solving it you know I I wonder is there  going to be a point of no return where  everyone keeps talking talking talking  and not doing anything and then it  becomes too late and then I think about  population at being at what seven  billion now I mean how many more people  can the earth carry right well that's  interesting I mean I think that there's  really a distinction between population  growth and climate change are somewhat  different because climate change  produces a kind of environmental  outcomes rising sea levels at  acidification of the ocean they're very  it's likely linked to extreme or extreme  weather there there's specific things  that come whereas I think population  growth is kind of results in the kind of  different kinds of diffuse pressures  that are felt by the marketplace and so  there are many different ways to solve  the problems that are the challenges the  population growth poses so I don't think  that we're gonna run to the point where  one more person or a certain number of  people are going to lead us over the  cliff on population but I think and  climate actually it is more that's a  little less clear in terms of how that's  going to unfold and and there are  possibly tipping points with the climate  and we don't entirely understand the  climate system and how it will develop  and so that's those are the kinds of  risks and uncertainties that the kind of  bet that we're making right now that we  need to address so how do we address my  mean what's the first step  well like as I was saying I think it's  really about putting a price on carbon  on trying to bring the the problems  associated with with global warming into  the into market framework where we can  be bringing the  kind of forces to bear on solving them  the kind of innovation that Julian Simon  celebrated will only happen if we can  create the proper incentives for people  to be innovating and taking action so I  think it's really about a partnership  between government and and the  marketplace and that the marketplace  needs to be structured in a way that if  it kind of furthers the values that we  have the kind of society we want to live  in well hopefully that will happen  sooner rather than constantly optimistic  but there are a lot of reasons not to be  - okay thank you so much for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  work thanks so much for having me for  more information about Professor Sabin  and h